<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Union of Brest</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1595">1595</a>–<a href="/years/1596">1596</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Union of Brest was the agreement of 1595-1596 by which most <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a> accepted the authority of the pope while keeping their Byzantine rite and customs. Confirmed at a synod in Brest in 1596, it created the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruthenian-uniate-church">Ruthenian Uniate Church</a>. It split the Orthodox population and caused lasting religious conflict that fed later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> revolts.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/union-of-brest.jpg" alt="Union of Brest" width="320" height="255" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Religions_in_Poland_1573.PNG: User:Mathiasrex on layers of User:Halibutt derivative work: Hoodinski · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Religie_w_I_Rz-plitej_1573.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
